hypotheses.statement:
Translate and dilate a convex body $K$ so that $0\in\operatorname{int}K$ and $|K|=\kappa_n$. Define $\eta_K$ by $$\label{eq:eta-definition}
 \int \psi(x)\,d\eta_K(x)
 =\mathbb E_{\sigma}\!\left[g_K(u)^{-n}\psi\bigl(\nabla g_K(u)\bigr)\right]$$ for nonnegative Borel functions $\psi$; the choice of the gradient on its null exceptional set is immaterial.

outputs.exact_statement:
For every integer $n\ge4$ and every convex body $K\subset\mathbb R^n$, $$\label{eq:main}
 \frac{|\Pi K|}{|K|^{n-1}}
 \ge \kappa_{n-1}^{\,n}\kappa_n^{\,2-n}.$$ Equality holds if and only if $K=a+TB_2^n$ for some $a\in\mathbb R^n$ and some invertible linear map $T$.

Let $s:S^{n-1}\to(0,\infty)$ and $\phi:S^{n-1}\to\mathbb R$ be continuous. For sufficiently small $|t|$, define $$W_t=\bigcap_{v\in S^{n-1}}\{x\in\mathbb R^n:x\cdot v\le s(v)+t\phi(v)\},
 \qquad g_t=g_{W_t}.$$ Then $W_t$ is a convex body containing the origin in its interior, and $$\label{eq:gauge-formulas}
 g_t(x)=\max_{v\in S^{n-1}}\frac{x\cdot v}{s(v)+t\phi(v)},
 \qquad
 \frac{|W_t|}{\kappa_n}=\mathbb E_{\sigma}g_t(u)^{-n}.$$ For almost every $u\in S^{n-1}$, the maximizing vector $v/s(v)$ at $t=0$ is unique and equals the Euclidean gradient $\nabla g_0(u)$. At these directions, $$\label{eq:gauge-derivative}
 \left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}g_t(u)
 =-g_0(u)\phi\bigl(\nabla g_0(u)\bigr).$$ On the sphere the gauges are uniformly bounded above and away from zero for small $|t|$, and the difference quotients $(g_t-g_0)/t$ are uniformly bounded. In particular, $$\label{eq:constraint-volume-derivative}
 \left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}|W_t|
 =n\kappa_n\mathbb E_{\sigma}\!\left[
   g_0(u)^{-n}\phi\bigl(\nabla g_0(u)\bigr)\right].$$ For $s=h_L$, one has $W_0=L$.

For every integer $d\ge1$ and convex bodies $A,B\subset\mathbb R^d$, write $|\cdot|$ for $d$-dimensional volume. For $0\le t\le1$, $$\label{eq:brunn-minkowski}
 |(1-t)A+tB|^{1/d}
 \ge (1-t)|A|^{1/d}+t|B|^{1/d}.$$ If $0<t<1$, equality holds if and only if $B=a+cA$ for some $a\in\mathbb R^d$ and $c>0$.

Translate and dilate a convex body $K$ so that $0\in\operatorname{int}K$ and $|K|=\kappa_n$. Define $\eta_K$ by $$\label{eq:eta-definition}
 \int \psi(x)\,d\eta_K(x)
 =\mathbb E_{\sigma}\!\left[g_K(u)^{-n}\psi\bigl(\nabla g_K(u)\bigr)\right]$$ for nonnegative Borel functions $\psi$; the choice of the gradient on its null exceptional set is immaterial. Then $\eta_K$ is an admissible probability measure of bounded support. Equality in <a href="#eq:functional-condition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:functional-condition">[eq:functional-condition]</a> for $\eta_K$ and a symmetric body $M$ holds if and only if $K$ is a translate of a positive dilate of $M$. Moreover, $$\label{eq:projection-measure}
 h_{\Pi K}(y)=\frac{n\kappa_n}{2}
                  \int |x\cdot y|\,d\eta_K(x)
 \qquad(y\in\mathbb R^n),$$ and $$\label{eq:eta-normalization}
 \int h_K(x)\,d\eta_K(x)=1.$$ For the unit ball, $\eta_{B_2^n}=\sigma$, and consequently $$\label{eq:ball-constant}
 \frac{n\kappa_n b_n}{2}=\kappa_{n-1}.$$

On $\mathcal H_{2j}$ the operators $P$ and $C$ are scalar multipliers, with $$\label{eq:transform-multipliers}
 P_{2j}=(-1)^j
 \frac{1\cdot3\cdots(2j-1)}{p(p+2)\cdots(p+2j-2)},
 \qquad
 C_{2j}=\frac{pP_{2j}}{p-\lambda_{2j}},$$ where empty products equal one. In particular, $$\label{eq:cosine-recurrence}
 \begin{gathered}
 C_0=1,\qquad C_2=\frac1{p+2},\qquad
 C_4=-\frac1{(p+2)(p+4)},\\[3pt]
 \frac{C_{l+2}}{C_l}=-\frac{l-1}{l+p+2}\quad(l\ge2\text{ even}).
 \end{gathered}$$

Let $k>0$ and let $f_1,f_2$ be even $C^1$ functions on $S^{n-1}$. If $f_1$ has zero projection onto $\mathcal H_2$, then $$\label{eq:sign-bound}
 \mathcal B(f_1,f_2)
 \ge (\mathbb E_{\sigma}f_1)(\mathbb E_{\sigma}f_2)
       -c_{n,k}\left\lVert Qf_1\right\rVert_2\left\lVert Qf_2\right\rVert_2.$$ The operator identity <a href="#eq:sign-operator" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:sign-operator">[eq:sign-operator]</a> and this estimate also hold when $f_i=g_i^k$ for arbitrary norms $g_i$ on $\mathbb R^n$, with the vector fields defined almost everywhere. In that case $$\label{eq:norm-vector-field}
 X_{g_i^k}(u)=g_i(u)^{k-1}\nabla g_i(u).$$

Let $g$ be a norm on $\mathbb R^n$, where $n\ge4$, normalized by $\mathbb E_{\sigma}g^{-n}=1$ on $S^{n-1}$. Then $$\label{eq:norm-estimate}
 c_{n,k}\|Q(g^k)\|_2^2
 \le (\mathbb E_{\sigma}g^k)^2-(\mathbb E_{\sigma}g^{k-1})^2.$$ The inequality is strict unless $g=1$ on $S^{n-1}$.

For every norm $g$ on $\mathbb R^n$, $$\label{eq:norm-curvature}
 2n\mathop{\mathrm{Var}}(G(g))+d_*\|QG(g)\|_2^2
 \le p\beta^2\mathbb E_{\sigma}[gI(g)],\qquad p=n-1.$$

The constants and functions above satisfy $$\label{eq:scalar-mean-bound}
 c\left(\frac{2n\beta^2}{wd_*}
       -\frac{(1-\beta)^2}{1-w}\right)
 \le\frac{2k-1}{k^2}$$ and $$\label{eq:scalar-pointwise}
 \mathcal L(z)\le2W(z)\qquad(z>0).$$ Equality in <a href="#eq:scalar-pointwise" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:scalar-pointwise">[eq:scalar-pointwise]</a> holds only at $z=1$.

Suppose $A$ is positive definite, and let $L$ be any positive dilate of $L_A$, with gauge $g=g_L$. For every continuous even function $\phi:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ that is positively homogeneous of degree one, $$\label{eq:representation}
 \int\phi(Ax)\,d\eta(x)
 =s\,\mathbb E_{\sigma}\phi\bigl(X_{g^k}(u)\bigr),
 \qquad
 s=\frac{\tau_A(L)}{\mathbb E_{\sigma}g^{k-1}}.$$ Here, almost everywhere on the sphere, $$\label{eq:norm-power-field}
 X_{g^k}=g^ku+g^{k-1}\nabla_Sg=g^{k-1}\nabla g.$$

Let $n\ge3$ and $1\le i\le n-1$ be integers, and let $K\subset\mathbb R^n$ be a convex body with nonempty interior. Then $$\label{eq:lutwak-petty}
\begin{aligned}
 \frac{V_{i+1}(\Pi_iK)}{V_{i+1}(K)^i}
 &\ge
 \frac{V_{i+1}(\Pi_iB_2^n)}{V_{i+1}(B_2^n)^i}\\
 &=
 \frac{\left(\displaystyle\binom{n-1}{i}
              \frac{\kappa_{n-1}}{\kappa_{n-i-1}}\right)^{i+1}}
      {\left(\displaystyle\binom{n}{i+1}
              \frac{\kappa_n}{\kappa_{n-i-1}}\right)^{i-1}}.
\end{aligned}$$ The lower bound is sharp, since $K=B_2^n$ attains it.

Let $n\ge3$ be an integer, put $q=n/(n-1)$, and let $f_1,\ldots,f_n\in C_c^1(\mathbb R^n)$ be nonnegative. Then $$\label{eq:mixed-affine-sobolev}
 \mathcal D_n(f_1,\ldots,f_n)
 \ge n!\,\kappa_{n-1}^{\,n}\kappa_n^{\,2-n}
       \prod_{i=1}^n\left\lVert f_i\right\rVert_q.$$ In particular, every nonnegative $f\in C_c^1(\mathbb R^n)$ satisfies the affine $L^1$ Sobolev inequality $$\label{eq:affine-sobolev}
 \mathcal D_n(f,\ldots,f)^{1/n}
 \ge (n!)^{1/n}\kappa_{n-1}\kappa_n^{(2-n)/n}\left\lVert f\right\rVert_q.$$ Both constants are sharp in this class, through smooth approximation of ellipsoid indicators in $BV(\mathbb R^n)$; no equality assertion for nonzero $C_c^1$ functions is made.

evidence.quote:
Let $T_{10}=\mathop{\mathrm{conv}}(0,e_1,\ldots,e_{10})\subset\mathbb R^{10}$, where the $e_i$ are the coordinate unit vectors, and let $K=T_{10}\times T_{10}\subset\mathbb R^{20}$. Then $$\frac{R_{20}(K)}{c_{20}}
 =\frac{121\binom{20}{10}}{21\cdot2^{20}}
 =\frac{22\,355\,476}{22\,020\,096}>1.$$ Thus $K$ violates <a href="#eq:proposed-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:proposed-bound">[eq:proposed-bound]</a> in dimension twenty.

